it checks the dirs are created after running (non-dry-run). handler.HumanLoop creates dirs via MkdirAll. Good.\n\nNow, one more consideration: the human-loop non-dry-run currently does NOT call env.Ensure. If I follow \"行为不变\", I keep it that way.\n\nLet me also reconsider: should handler.HumanLoop return the sessionID persistence? The current code doesn't persist sessionID for human-loop. The task detail mentions \"持久化 sessionID\" but the current code doesn't. And CallCLI returns only error (no sessionID). So I CANNOT persist sessionID from CallCLI (it doesn't return one). So \"持久化 sessionID\" is aspirational and not implementable with the current CallCLI signature.\n\nThis confirms: I should follow \"行为不变\" (behavior unchanged) and NOT add sessionID persistence (impossible with current CallCLI).\n\nSo my final design for handler.HumanLoop:\n```go\nfunc HumanLoop(topic string, opts Options) error {\n    draftDir, rfcDir, loopDir, err := prepareHumanLoopDirs()\n    if err != nil { return err }\n    cfg, err := config.LoadConfig()\n    if err != nil { return fmt.Errorf(\"failed to load config: %w\", err) }\n    pb := builder.NewPIBuilder()\n    mainFile, _, _, _, _, err := pb.SaveHumanLoopPrompt(topic, rfcDir, draftDir, loopDir)\n    if err != nil { return fmt.Errorf(\"failed to generate human-loop prompt: %w\", err) }\n    if opts.Verbose {\n        fmt.Printf(\"[INFO] Loop directory: %s\\n\", loopDir)\n        fmt.Printf(\"[INFO] Human-loop prompt saved to: %s\\n\", mainFile)\n        fmt.Printf(\"[INFO] rfc directory: %s\\n\", rfcDir)\n    }\n    if err := runtime.CallCLI(opts.Verbose, cfg, mainFile, runtime.ModeInteractive); err != nil {\n        return fmt.Errorf(\"failed to start pi CLI: %w\", err)\n    }\n    fmt.Printf(\"思考记录已保存到 %s\\n\", loopDir)\n    return nil\n}\n```\n\nAnd HumanLoopDryRun:\n```go\nfunc HumanLoopDryRun(topic string) error {\n    draftDir, rfcDir, _, err := prepareHumanLoopDirs()\n    if err != nil { return err }\n    _, content, err := builder.NewPIBuilder().BuildHumanLoop(topic, map[string]string{\"rfc_dir\": rfcDir, \"draft_dir\": draftDir})\n    if err != nil { return fmt.Errorf(\"failed to generate human-loop prompt: %w\", err) }\n    fmt.Print(content)\n    return nil\n}\n```\n\nWhere prepareHumanLoopDirs does:\n```go\nfunc prepareHumanLoopDirs() (draftDir, rfcDir, loopDir string, err error) {\n    draftDir, err = workspace.GetDraftDir()\n    if err != nil { return \"\", \"\", \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"failed to get draft directory: %w\", err) }\n    rfcDir = filepath.Join(draftDir, \"rfc\")\n    loopID, err := workspace.NextLoopID(draftDir)\n    if err != nil { return \"\", \"\", \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"failed to allocate loop id: %w\", err) }\n    loopDir = filepath.Join(draftDir, \"loops\", loopID)\n    for _, sub := range []string{draftDir, rfcDir, filepath.Join(draftDir, \"concepts\"), filepath.Join(draftDir, \"human-learning\"), filepath.Join(draftDir, \"loops\")} {\n        if err := os.MkdirAll(sub, 0755); err != nil { return \"\", \"\", \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"failed to create directory %s: %w\", sub, err) }\n    }\n    return draftDir, rfcDir, loopDir, nil\n}\n```\n\nWait, but the current code uses `rfcDir := draftDir + \"/rfc\"` (string concat). I'll use filepath.Join for cleanliness (equivalent on Linux).\n\nNow, the topic validation: the current cli does `if len(args) == 0 || args[0] == \"\"`. The task says \"cli 保留 NewHumanLoopCmd（topic 校验：空则报「topic is required」）\". So topic validation stays in the cli. Good.\n\nNow for the ctrl handler:\n```go\nfunc Ctrl(opts Options) error {\n    jID := opts.JobID\n    if jID == \"\" { return fmt.Errorf(\"--job flag is required (e.g. rick ctrl --job job_1)\") }\n    rickDir, err := workspace.GetRickDir()\n    ...\n    promptFile, _, err := builder.NewPIBuilder().SaveCtrlPrompt(jID, rickDir)\n    ...\n    cfg, err := config.LoadConfig()\n    ...\n    fmt.Printf(\"Job: %s\\n\", jID)\n    fmt.Println(\"🎮 Starting ctrl interactive session...\")\n    if err := runtime.CallCLI(opts.Verbose, cfg, promptFile, runtime.ModeInteractive); err != nil {\n        return fmt.Errorf(\"ctrl session failed: %w\", err)\n    }\n    return nil\n}\n\nfunc CtrlDryRun(opts Options) error {\n    jID := opts.JobID\n    if jID == \"\" { return fmt.Errorf(\"--job flag is required (e.g. rick ctrl --dry-run --job job_1)\") }\n    rickDir, err := workspace.GetRickDir()\n    ...\n    promptFile, _, err := builder.NewPIBuilder().SaveCtrlPrompt(jID, rickDir)\n    ...\n    data, err := os.ReadFile(promptFile)\n    ...\n    fmt.Printf(\"[DRY-RUN] ctrl prompt for %s:\\n\\n\", jID)\n    fmt.Print(string(data))\n    return nil\n}\n```\n\nWait, but the current ctrl dry-run does `SaveCtrlPrompt` which WRITES the prompt file AND returns the path, then reads it back. Hmm, that's a bit odd (writes then reads). Let me preserve it (behavior unchanged). Actually, I could use BuildCtrl + write, but to preserve behavior, I'll keep SaveCtrlPrompt + ReadFile.\n\nActually wait, there's a subtlety: `SaveCtrlPrompt` (in builder/pibuilder.go) calls `prompt.GenerateCtrlPromptFile` which:\n1. Stats doingDir → error \"doing directory not found for job %s: %s\" if not exists.\n2. Reads tasks.json, generates prompt, writes to doing/prompts/ctrl_prompt.md.\n\nSo SaveCtrlPrompt WRITES the file. Then handler reads it back. The test \"ctrl --dry-run --job job_N (doing dir doesn't exist)\" expects \"doing directory not found\" — this comes from SaveCtrlPrompt. Good.\n\nSo CtrlDryRun preserves the \"doing directory not found\" behavior via SaveCtrlPrompt. \n\nBut wait — the task says ctrl dry-run should report \"doing directory not found\" when doing dir doesn't exist. The current `runCtrlDryRun` returns the error from SaveCtrlPrompt (which is \"doing directory not found for job %s\"). So the handler should return this error (not swallow it). Let me make sure CtrlDryRun returns the error.\n\nActually, looking at the current runCtrlDryRun:\n```go\nfunc runCtrlDryRun() error {\n    jID := GetJobID()\n    if jID == \"\" { return fmt.Errorf(\"--job flag is required ...\") }\n    rickDir, err := workspace.GetRickDir()\n    if err != nil { return fmt.Errorf(\"failed to get rick directory: %w\", err) }\n    promptFile, _, err := builder.NewPIBuilder().SaveCtrlPrompt(jID, rickDir)\n    if err != nil { return fmt.Errorf(\"failed to generate ctrl prompt: %w\", err) }\n    data, err := os.ReadFile(promptFile)\n    ...\n}\n```\n\nSo SaveCtrlPrompt error (\"doing directory not found...\") is wrapped as \"failed to generate ctrl prompt: doing directory not found...\". The test checks for \"doing directory not found\" substring, which is still present in the wrapped error. Good.\n\nOK. Now for the dream handler:\n```go\nfunc Dream(jobNum int, background bool, opts Options) error {\n    // migrate dreamWorkflow\n}\nfunc DreamDryRun(jobNum int) error {\n    // migrate runDreamDryRun, using workspace.SelectPendingJobs\n}\n```\n\nThe dream scan functions move to workspace. So handler.Dream calls `workspace.SelectPendingJobs(rickDir, jobNum)`.\n\nLet me write the dream handler:\n\n```go\nfunc DreamDryRun(jobNum int) error {\n    rickDir, err := workspace.GetRickDir()\n    if err != nil {\n        fmt.Printf(\"[DRY-RUN] failed to get rick dir: %v\\n\", err)\n        return nil\n    }\n    jobIDs := workspace.SelectPendingJobs(rickDir, jobNum)\n    fmt.Printf(\"[DRY-RUN] Pending jobs (%d): %v\\n\", len(jobIDs), jobIDs)\n    _, content, err := builder.NewPIBuilder().BuildDream(map[string]string{\n        \"rick_dir\": rickDir,\n        \"job_ids\":  strings.Join(jobIDs, \",\"),\n    })\n    if err != nil {\n        fmt.Printf(\"[DRY-RUN] failed to generate dream prompt: %v\\n\", err)\n        return nil\n    }\n    fmt.Printf(\"[DRY-RUN] Dream prompt:\\n\\n\")\n    fmt.Print(content)\n    return nil\n}\n```\n\n```go\nfunc Dream(jobNum int, background bool, opts Options) error {\n    fmt.Println(\"\\n=== Dream Workflow ===\")\n    rickDir, err := workspace.GetRickDir()\n    if err != nil { return fmt.Errorf(\"failed to get rick directory: %w\", err) }\n    dreamDir := filepath.Join(rickDir, workspace.DreamDirName)\n    if err := os.MkdirAll(dreamDir, 0755); err != nil { return fmt.Errorf(\"failed to create dream directory: %w\", err) }\n    jobIDs := workspace.SelectPendingJobs(rickDir, jobNum)\n    if len(jobIDs) == 0 {\n        fmt.Println(\"No new completed jobs to process.\")\n        fmt.Println(\"All jobs have already been dreamed, or no jobs are fully completed yet.\")\n        return nil\n    }\n    fmt.Printf(\"Processing %d job(s): %s\\n\", len(jobIDs), strings.Join(jobIDs, \", \"))\n    promptFile, _, err := builder.NewPIBuilder().SaveDreamPrompt(jobIDs, rickDir)\n    if err != nil { return fmt.Errorf(\"failed to generate dream prompt: %w\", err) }\n    cfg, err := config.LoadConfig()\n    if err != nil { return fmt.Errorf(\"failed to load config: %w\", err) }\n    if background {\n        fmt.Println(\"🤖 Starting pi (background mode)...\")\n        if err := runtime.CallCLI(opts.Verbose, cfg, promptFile, runtime.ModePrint); err != nil { return fmt.Errorf(\"pi failed: %w\", err) }\n    } else {\n        fmt.Println(\"🤖 Starting pi (interactive mode)...\")\n        if err := runtime.CallCLI(opts.Verbose, cfg, promptFile, runtime.ModeInteractive); err != nil { return fmt.Errorf(\"pi failed: %w\", err) }\n    }\n    fmt.Println(\"\\n✅ Dream phase completed!\")\n    fmt.Println(\"Agent should have written dream_run_{job_id}_log.md for each processed job.\")\n    return nil\n}\n```\n\nNote: the current dreamWorkflow uses `GetVerbose()` for CallCLI. I'll use `opts.Verbose`.\n\nNow for the learning handler:\n```go\ntype ExecutionData struct {\n    JobID        string\n    DebugContent string\n    TasksJSON    *executor.TasksJSON\n    TaskMDPaths  []string\n    RickDir      string\n    ActPathFiles []string\n}\n\nfunc Learning(jobID string, opts Options) error {\n    // migrate executeLearningWorkflow\n}\n\nfunc LearningDryRun(job