{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Task7 is already marked as success with commit d184e12 (the previous feat commit). My fix commit af47384 is a follow-up. The tasks.json still points to d184e12 for task7.\n\nShould I update the commit_hash for task7 to my new commit af47384? Let me think. The task7 commit_hash is d184e12, which was the \"feat(task7)\" commit. My fix is af47384.\n\nActually, the doing_check passed, so the current state is acceptable. But it might be better to update the commit_hash to reflect my fix. However, updating tasks.json is a \"mark task success\" operation that's typically done by the pipeline.\n\nActually, looking more carefully — the doing_check passed with \"8/12 tasks succeeded\". task7 is marked success. The commit hash points to d184e12. \n\nHmm, but wait — should I update the commit hash to my fix commit? The `mark-task-success` skill says: \"doing task 代码已提交（有 commit hash）但 doing_check 报错\". That's for when doing_check errors.\n\nLet me check the check-mechanism or mark-task-success skill to understand. Actually, the doing_check passed already. So there's no error. My fix commit just needs to be there.\n\nBut actually, is it important that the task7 commit_hash points to my fix commit? Let me think about what's the right thing. The commit_hash in tasks.json is used to verify that the task's code was actually committed. Since the previous commit d184e12 already contained the task7 work (just with a naming issue), and my fix af47384 is an additional commit, the \"canonical\" commit for task7 could be either.\n\nActually, the cleaner thing would be to update the commit_hash to af47384 since that's the latest commit that completes task7. But this might be overstepping — the tasks.json is usually updated by the harness/pipeline, not by me manually.\n\nLet me check the check-mechanism skill and mark-task-success skill to understand what I should do.\n\nActually, let me re-read the Doing Loop COMMIT step:\n\"1. `git add` + `git commit`（commit message 含 task ID）\n2. 运行 check 命令... check 失败 → 修复后重新运行，循环直到 pass\"\n\nSo I just need to commit and run check. The check passed. I don't need to manually update tasks.json.\n\nBut wait — the tasks.json already has task7 marked success with commit d184e12. My new commit af47384 is a fix on top. Should the commit_hash be updated?\n\nLet me look at the mark-task-success skill and check-mechanism skill to understand the convention."}}
