{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"The previous \"chore(task7): mark task7 success\" commit b46de75 only touched tasks.json, NOT bin/rick. So bin/rick was left uncommitted (dirty) after the feat commit d184e12.\n\nThe feat commit d184e12 DID commit bin/rick (built to 8393570 bytes). But then something rebuilt bin/rick again (to a different content at same size), leaving it dirty.\n\nActually wait — let me trace:\n1. d184e12 committed bin/rick at 8393570 bytes.\n2. b46de75 committed only tasks.json.\n3. My session started with bin/rick showing \" M\" (modified) with diff \"8393570 -> 8393570 bytes\".\n\nSo between d184e12 and my session, someone/something rebuilt bin/rick (the previous attempt's test run may have done `go build -o bin/rick`). The rebuild produced a different binary at the same size.\n\nNow I rebuilt it AGAIN with my latest code (which includes the workspace naming fix). This produces yet another binary.\n\nThe question: should I commit bin/rick?\n\nGiven:\n1. bin/rick is tracked (force-added in past).\n2. .gitignore has bin/ ignored.\n3. Previous commits mixed: feat commit included bin/rick, chore commits didn't.\n\nI think the right call is to NOT force-add bin/rick, since:\n- It's gitignored (bin/)\n- It's a build artifact\n- My code change is already committed (af47384) and the binary reflects it after rebuild\n- The acceptance tests build their own binary to temp dir\n\nBut leaving bin/rick \"dirty\" is also not ideal. However, since the .gitignore says bin/ should be ignored, the \"dirty\" bin/rick is arguably a pre-existing situation not caused by me.\n\nActually, the cleanest and most defensible: commit the rebuilt bin/rick with -f to match the repo's actual convention (the feat commit included it). This keeps the tracked binary in sync with the source.\n\nLet me do `git add -f bin/rick` and commit.\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider. My rebuild of bin/rick was with `go build -o bin/rick ./cmd/rick`. This is the correct build. The binary now reflects my fix. Committing it with -f keeps the repo consistent.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: is the binary deterministic? Go builds are generally deterministic for the same source and toolchain, but not guaranteed. The previous dirty state (8393570 -> 8393570) suggests a rebuild produced different content. My rebuild is another one.\n\nI'll commit it with -f to keep the tracked binary in sync. This is consistent with the feat commit's behavior.\n\nLet me do it."}}
